ing this up  Stephen actually had a blog a little  while back and I'll have there's a link  to that in the talk here where he has  kind of a 10-step process for how to  clean data to make it computable and  that's good good good tips to follow by  so the first thing we did was we had to  import the CSV file and you can see here  the from the header the CSV file some of  the properties that are provided in the  data set that they give us we've also  looked at other marathons not just  Champaign but we've looked at a/c  Philadelphia Pittsburgh we've done  Houston we've kind of done these as  background things and they all pretty  much have basically the same properties  we have to tweak things a little bit on  a per race basis but one thing in common  is that they're all very messy you have  to do a lot of cleanup to make the data  computable one of the best tools that  you need to use is interpreter the data  comes in as a string but if you want to  compute with it strings are kind of hard  to compute with but the Wolfram language  has a lot of entities in them  you want to convert a lot of these  things into entities things like this  city where the runner came from their  zip codes their gender these are all  things that the Wolfram language has a  representation of as an entity and so  how do you turn these strings into one  of these entities and figure out what it  is once you've done that you can  actually get access to things properties  and city data  you know the actual latitude longitude  of those cities things that you can  actually compute with but you've got to  align them first and that's where  interpreter comes in scroll down a  little here so here's an example where  from the CSV file we're able to get  their city their state in the country  put those together and string join them  and then you can call interpreter city  on it and what you get back is the  entity representation so each one of  these calls essentially goes through a  Wolfram Alpha parser and then turns it  into the internal representation now the  problem is this can be messy as I  mentioned before is all data can be  here's an example of something that  might fail so you always have to have  fallback plans there's never just that  this will always work for you usually  have to make multiple passes to clean  things up so here's a case where  somebody put in for their city Scott Air  Force Base it's not really a city we've  seen all kinds of weird things  especially in the city fields people  will put in their address they don't  actually put the city they put an  address in they put an Air Force Base  they put a neighborhood that's a subtle  differentiation they might say Queens  rather than New York but from a an  alignment point of view it's actually  it's a difference so these can all throw  off an attempt to align these things so  often what we were able to do here was  we were able to fall back to their zip  code in the event we couldn't figure out  what city they were from and postal code  is provided so you're able to do that  and then we can call geo nearest on the  resulting city to determine what they  are and it turns out we actually did  have Scott Air Force Base right there  and we were actually getting able to get  the alignment sometimes these  misalignments because the thing they  provided aren't a city sometimes it's  because they misspelled it sometimes  it's because we're lacking when  linguistics and so the  or sometimes cues for us to add  linguistics to our stuff to pick some of  these up but there are all things that  you're trying to clean up the day be  you've got to be prepared for that's it  okay one of the biggest things most of  the data that you really get out there  that you really care about are the the  split times so the champagne marathon  had several different splits of course  there's the start then there's the 10k  the 10-mile the half the 20-mile and the  finish some of the other marathons  actually have mor